[Veto] {
  "title": "fix: reject zero divisor in divide()",
  "body": "## Summary\n- `divide(a, b)` in `example.js` now throws `Error(\"Cannot divide by zero\")` when `b === 0`.\n- Before this change it returned `a / b` unguarded, so a zero divisor gave `Infinity`, `-Infinity`, or `NaN` without any error.\n- The function body moved from a one-liner to a multi-line block to fit the guard. For non-zero divisors the return value is unchanged.\n\n## Changes\n- **example.js** (+4 / -1): added a `b === 0` guard that throws before the division and reformatted `divide` across multiple lines.\n\n## Test Plan\n- [ ] `divide(6, 3)` returns `2`.\n- [ ] `divide(1, 0)` throws `Error` with message `\"Cannot divide by zero\"`.\n- [ ] `divide(0, 0)` throws instead of returning `NaN`.\n- [ ] `divide(1, -0)` throws, since `-0 === 0` is true.\n- [ ] `divide(-5, 2)` returns `-2.5` (non-zero divisors unaffected).\n- [ ] Non-number divisors such as `divide(1, \"0\")` are not caught by the strict `=== 0` check. Confirm this is the intended behavior.\n\n## Breaking Changes\n- Callers that passed `0` as the divisor used to get `Infinity`/`-Infinity`/`NaN` back. They now get a thrown `Error`. Any caller that relied on those values or doesn't catch exceptions must be updated.",
  "base_branch": "main",
  "commits": [
    "2d0c138 fix: reject zero divisor"
  ],
